            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the standard deviation - how far the values typically are from their mean, in the same units as the values. It measures how spread out values are: how jittery a sensor is when still, how much a joint moves over a window, how varied the dancers' positions are.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.std <dim>\nWithout a dim, the whole tensor gives one number. With one, a dim box and a keep_dims\ncheckbox appear, and each slice along the dim gets its own (t.std 0 on frames x joints\ngives one per joint).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim measured along.\n\nkeep_dims (checkbox, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nKeep the measured dim, as size 1, so the answer lines up with the input.\n\ncorrection:\nAn option, under the node. 1 (torch's default) divides by one less than the number of values - the estimate of a whole population's spread from a sample of it. 0 divides by the number of values, as np.std and np.var do; so the two disagree unless correction is 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe standard deviation(s).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2 4 4 4 5 5 7 9 has mean 5. With correction 1 the standard deviation is 2.138;\nwith correction 0 it is 2.\n\nRELATED:\nt.var gives the square of it. np.std and np.var are the numpy versions. t.mean gives the mean. scipy.zscore measures each value in standard deviations from the mean.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1050,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the standard deviation - how far the values typically are from their mean, in the same units as the values. It measures how spread out values are: how jittery a sensor is when still, how much a joint moves over a window, how varied the dancers' positions are.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.std <dim>\nWithout a dim, the whole tensor gives one number. With one, a dim box and a keep_dims\ncheckbox appear, and each slice along the dim gets its own (t.std 0 on frames x joints\ngives one per joint).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim measured along.\n\nkeep_dims (checkbox, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nKeep the measured dim, as size 1, so the answer lines up with the input.\n\ncorrection:\nAn option, under the node. 1 (torch's default) divides by one less than the number of values - the estimate of a whole population's spread from a sample of it. 0 divides by the number of values, as np.std and np.var do; so the two disagree unless correction is 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe standard deviation(s).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2 4 4 4 5 5 7 9 has mean 5. With correction 1 the standard deviation is 2.138;\nwith correction 0 it is 2.\n\nRELATED:\nt.var gives the square of it. np.std and np.var are the numpy versions. t.mean gives the mean. scipy.zscore measures each value in standard deviations from the mean."
        },
